# Access control lists for C-03. FR-14, FR-004 and ADR-0008 in one file.
#
# The rule that matters: a client is granted what its role's block names and nothing else.
# Mosquitto denies by default, so an omission here is a denial rather than a hole, and a
# role that has been given no block can do nothing at all once it has authenticated.
#
# Roles, not client instances. Ten sensors share one role, so adding a sensor needs no rule
# and gains no permission. Credentials for each role are produced at deploy time and appear
# in no tracked file.
#
# The two namespaces are conventions of the harness rather than configuration:
# obs/<thing-id>/<datastream-id> carries observations, and the declared ctl/ topics carry
# control events. Nothing else is in use, and this file is what makes that true rather than
# customary. Cross-contamination of flows is C-03's named failure mode (SRD §4), and these
# rules are the only thing preventing it — so they are tested at the broker, by attempting
# each forbidden publish and subscription and asserting the refusal.

# --- sensors (C-04) --------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Publish under obs/, announce liveness on ctl/heartbeat, and nothing else. A sensor that
# could publish on ctl/ generally could forge a divergence event or a run request, so the
# branch is refused as a branch rather than by convention in the sensor's source: a
# convention is not a control. Two named topics are the exceptions, and each is argued for
# below rather than assumed.
#
# The write is ctl/heartbeat, and it was added on 27 August 2026 (ADR-0015) after wiring
# every component to a real broker showed what its absence cost. Until then this file gave
# the sensor role no write outside obs/, so its heartbeat was refused at the broker — and
# refused silently, because a client library reports success for a message it has accepted
# locally and handed on. The component believed it had announced itself, nothing had heard
# anything, and C-04 could never light its box in the shell however it was wired.
#
# The objection this file used to make to the write was forgery: a heartbeat names its own
# component, so any role that can publish one can claim to be any component. That objection
# is true and is not held anywhere else. drogna_control below carries `readwrite ctl/#`,
# which is nine components already able to forge any heartbeat including a sensor's. The
# exclusion therefore bought no property the file actually holds, and it cost the browser
# client a permanent untruth about a component that was running correctly — in the one
# display whose whole purpose is that a box lights only when something was genuinely heard
# from. A false negative in that display is worse than a forgery risk already accepted for
# nine other roles.
#
# What the write does not extend to: ctl/divergence, ctl/run-request, ctl/run-started,
# ctl/run-published, ctl/plan and ctl/telemetry are all still refused to this role, and
# tests/integration/test_topic_isolation.py asserts each refusal against a running broker.
#
# The one read is the clock sample, and it is an exception this file has to argue for.
# FR-14 as written gives sensors no subscription to ctl/ at all. ADR-0009 then decided that
# simulation time reaches every component by subscription to ctl/clock, the browser
# included. A sensor that cannot receive a clock sample has no simulation time, and a
# component with no simulation time can only pace itself on the host clock, which
# Constitution I forbids outright. So the sensor role is given exactly one control topic —
# the time — and refused every other, in both directions. The property to test is therefore
# not that a sensor cannot subscribe to ctl/#; it is that subscribing to ctl/# delivers it
# the clock and nothing else, which is what tests/integration/test_topic_isolation.py
# asserts against a running broker. deploy/broker/README.md records the conflict and this
# reading of it.

user drogna_sensor
topic write obs/#
topic write ctl/heartbeat
topic read ctl/clock

# --- ingest client (C-05) ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Reads the observation branch, and publishes its own liveness and its own degradation.
# Nothing else: the single ingestion seam has no business raising a divergence event or
# commanding a model run, and the two write topics below are the only ones it is given.
#
# It reads the clock sample for the same reason the sensors do — a component with no
# simulation time can only pace itself on the host clock — and reads no other control
# topic.

user drogna_ingest
topic read obs/#
topic read ctl/clock
topic write ctl/heartbeat
topic write ctl/telemetry

# --- control components (C-01, C-11 to C-17) --------------------------------------------
#
# The control namespace, in both directions, plus read access to the observation branch:
# the monitor subscribes to obs/# directly and derives sound speed at the point of use
# (ADR-0005), rather than querying the store during normal operation.
#
# Write access to obs/ is deliberately absent. A control component that could publish an
# observation could put a value into the store that no sensor measured, which is the same
# contamination as the sensor case in the other direction.

user drogna_control
topic readwrite ctl/#
topic read obs/#

# --- the query layer (C-09) --------------------------------------------------------------
#
# One write and nothing else. C-09 serves reads over HTTP and touches the broker for exactly
# one purpose: `query/plugins/heartbeat.py` announces that it is alive, so that its box in
# the shell is lit by a message rather than by a configuration file saying it exists. It
# subscribes to nothing — freshness reaches it through the query path, not the control
# branch — so it is given no read rule at all.
#
# It is a role of its own rather than `drogna_control`, which carries `readwrite ctl/#`.
# Handing a read-only query surface write access to the whole control namespace would let
# the component that answers questions raise a divergence event or command a model run,
# which is the cross-contamination C-03 owns as its failure mode. It is not `drogna_viewer`
# either: that identity has no write rule by design (ADR-0008) and giving it one would
# quietly grant the browser a voice.
#
# Added 27 August 2026, when the tracked broker URLs first named the roles they authenticate
# as and this component turned out to name none that fitted (ADR-0016).

user drogna_query
topic write ctl/heartbeat

# --- the browser (C-18), through the proxy's upgrade location ---------------------------
#
# ADR-0008: subscribe-only on the control namespace, incapable of publishing anywhere, and
# not confusable with a sensor identity — it is a separate role with a separate credential,
# and it has no write rule at all.
#
# It is given no access to obs/. Observation traffic is not proxied to the browser; the
# client reads observations through the query layer, as FR-19 intends. A viewer that could
# subscribe to obs/# would be reading the write path over a connection the proxy does not
# inspect per message.

user drogna_viewer
topic read ctl/#

# --- the topic tree (C-18), through the same upgrade location -----------------------------
#
# ADR-0027: the topic-tree panel (feature 022) draws the declared topology lit by live
# traffic, and a tree restricted to the control namespace would misrepresent the system it
# exists to demonstrate. So the browser gains a second identity: read on both namespaces,
# and — the property that makes the credential publishable, exactly as ADR-0020 argued for
# the viewer — *publish on nothing at all*. Mosquitto's default deny supplies every refusal;
# tests/integration/test_topic_isolation.py asserts the grants and the refusals at a running
# broker rather than reading this file back.
#
# drogna_viewer above is unchanged and keeps ADR-0020's obligations: it remains the narrow
# identity for anything needing only the control namespace, and it may never gain a
# permission. This role carries the same second obligation for the same reason: its secret
# is rendered into the world-readable client document, so a write rule added here would turn
# a documented non-secret into a real one. The objection ADR-0008 raised against the viewer
# reading obs/# — the write path read over a connection the proxy does not inspect per
# message — is answered rather than waved off: the connection still cannot write (this file),
# the observation payloads are the synthetic measurements of a demonstration harness, and
# the released products behind the proxy's clearance are a different credential on a
# different route, untouched (ADR-0027 records the argument in full).

user drogna_observer
topic read obs/#
topic read ctl/#
